# The control for the KASAN experiment — the same drivers built in, no KASAN.
#
#   KERNEL_CONFIG=builtin-drivers build/build.sh kernel
#       -> build/out/h713-kernel-builtin-drivers.fit
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The mpv-on-panel crash (docs/vaapi-scope.md) stopped
# reproducing on the KASAN kernel: 540 seconds of playback, ~16,000 frames, no
# crash and no report, where the production kernel failed 3 of 4 runs inside 48
# seconds. But that kernel changed TWO things at once, unavoidably — KASAN only
# instruments what is compiled in its tree, so covering the four suspect drivers
# meant building them in. So a clean run has two explanations, and they call for
# opposite next moves:
#
#   * KASAN's 2-3x slowdown moved a timing-sensitive race out of its window
#     -> instrument more cheaply (DEBUG_OBJECTS_WORK targets the exact
#        signature: a work item whose function pointer is NULL)
#   * built-in vs module is itself the variable
#     -> the bug lives in module-shaped memory, and the next KASAN kernel wants
#        those drivers back as instrumented modules in /lib/modules/6.18.38-builtin
#
# This config removes KASAN and keeps everything else, so the run that follows
# distinguishes them. A crash here means KASAN was masking it; a clean run here
# means the module path is implicated.
#
# Deliberately standalone rather than composed with kasan.config: both fragments
# set CONFIG_LOCALVERSION, and the merge would silently let one win.

CONFIG_VIDEO_SUNXI_CEDRUS=y
CONFIG_DRM_SUN50I_H713_AFBD=y
CONFIG_DRM_PANFROST=y
CONFIG_SUNXI_SCANOUT_DMABUF=y

# Kept from kasan.config on purpose. It is inert unless something types a
# sysrq, so it does not muddy the comparison, and being able to get a wedged
# board back without walking to the power switch has already paid for itself
# three times this session.
CONFIG_MAGIC_SYSRQ=y

# uname -r becomes 6.18.38-builtin, so /lib/modules/6.18.38-builtin does not
# exist and udev loads nothing. Required, not cosmetic: the production module
# tree still holds cedrus/afbd/panfrost/scanout-dmabuf built for 6.18.38, and
# loading those on top of the same drivers now built in would double-register
# them. It also keeps this kernel's module tree distinct from the KASAN one.
CONFIG_LOCALVERSION="-builtin"
